WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.140
Photoreceptors release one transmitter, glutamate.

2
00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.660
More in the dark, less in the light.

3
00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:13.620
Yet some bipolar cells depolarize to light, and others hyperpolarize.

4
00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:15.500
How?

5
00:00:15.783 --> 00:00:19.083
Picture one cone terminal feeding two bipolar cells.

6
00:00:19.963 --> 00:00:20.963
On the left, darkness.

7
00:00:21.923 --> 00:00:22.923
On the right, light.

8
00:00:23.783 --> 00:00:27.483
The OFF bipolar cell has ionotropic glutamate receptors.

9
00:00:28.483 --> 00:00:32.743
Glutamate opens their cation channels, so in the dark the cell is depolarized.

10
00:00:33.667 --> 00:00:38.347
Light lowers glutamate, so the excitation fades, and the cell hyperpolarizes.

11
00:00:39.287 --> 00:00:40.287
The sign is kept.

12
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:45.480
The ON bipolar cell uses a different receptor, a metabotropic one.

13
00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:49.900
In the dark, glutamate on this receptor activates a G protein.

14
00:00:50.983 --> 00:00:53.783
That G protein holds a cation channel shut.

15
00:00:54.523 --> 00:00:57.143
So in the dark, the ON cell is hyperpolarized.

16
00:00:58.033 --> 00:00:59.313
Light lowers glutamate.

17
00:01:00.133 --> 00:01:04.353
The G protein's activity falls, the brake comes off, and the channel opens.

18
00:01:05.313 --> 00:01:06.413
The cell depolarizes.

19
00:01:07.353 --> 00:01:08.393
The sign is inverted.

20
00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:10.927
Keep a ledger of the flips.

21
00:01:11.827 --> 00:01:13.567
The photoreceptor flips the sign once.

22
00:01:14.427 --> 00:01:15.827
The OFF cell adds no flip.

23
00:01:16.687 --> 00:01:17.927
The ON cell adds a second.

24
00:01:18.917 --> 00:01:22.217
An even number of flips means the cell responds like light.

25
00:01:23.037 --> 00:01:24.077
That makes it an ON cell.

26
00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:26.980
How do we know the receptors differ?

27
00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:31.640
Slaughter and Miller recorded inside cells of the mudpuppy retina.

28
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.800
They bathed the retina in a glutamate mimic that acts on the ON receptor.

29
00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:39.810
Add the drug, then turn on a light.

30
00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:41.710
What does the ON cell do?

31
00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:43.870
And the OFF cell?

32
00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:50.820
The ON responses vanished.

33
00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:55.840
The drug held the ON cells in their dark state, so light had nothing to remove.

34
00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.730
The OFF responses carried on, in the same retina.

35
00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:03.350
So the two cells read glutamate with different receptors.

36
00:02:04.483 --> 00:02:08.003
Each cone feeds two streams, one for lighter, one for darker.

37
00:02:08.863 --> 00:02:12.043
The takeaway: the receptor, not the molecule, sets the sign.
